## Source task

For each of three specified pairs of sets, decide whether either set is a
subset of the other. Both directions must be examined; the written task does
not stipulate that exactly one direction must hold.

### 62(a): finite sets

$A=\{-1,0,3\}$ and $B=\{-4,-3,-1,0,2,3,4\}$.

Every element of A occurs in B: -1, 0 and 3 are all listed in B. Therefore
$A\subseteq B$. The reverse is false because $2\in B$ but $2\notin A$
(also -4, -3 and 4 are witnesses). Consequently $A\ne B$ and A is a proper
subset of B. Only the A-to-B direction holds.

### 62(b): integers and even numbers

$A=Z$ and $B=\{2k:k\in Z\}$.

For any $x\in B$, there is an integer k with $x=2k$, hence x is an integer.
Thus $B\subseteq A$. The reverse is false: $1\in Z$ is not an even number.
Consequently B is a proper subset of A. Only the B-to-A direction holds.
Zero and negative even numbers remain even integers; positivity was not
introduced into either set.

### 62(c): fractions and positive numbers

The permitted book definition fixes $A=Q\setminus Z$, not all numbers written
with a fraction bar. In particular integers remain excluded regardless of
their notation. B is the specified positive-number set.

$-1/2$ is rational and nonintegral, so $-1/2\in A$, but it is negative and
therefore not in B. This refutes $A\subseteq B$.

$1$ is positive, so $1\in B$, but it is integral and therefore not in
$Q\setminus Z$. This refutes $B\subseteq A$.

Neither set contains the other. This conclusion does not require assuming
whether the positive-number set includes irrational numbers: the witnesses
-1/2 and 1 settle both directions under the stated source conditions.
The sets are not disjoint: $1/2$ belongs to both. Noncontainment must not be
confused with absence of shared elements.

## Blind result and later comparison standard

The six directed inclusion truth values, in a/b/c order, are:
A-to-B true, B-to-A false; A-to-B false, B-to-A true;
A-to-B false, B-to-A false.

A faithful recognition unit can combine these three comparisons, provided
it retains both directions and the book's fraction definition. Its learner
response must not be called a completed proof. Selecting supplied conclusions
cannot establish that the learner independently constructs the elementwise
argument, the quantified even-integer argument, or the two counterexamples.
Those production losses must be explicitly assessed in the later review.
